            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Leibnizian Monadology</strong><br>\nLeibniz's monadology introduced the idea that the universe is made up of an infinite number of simple substances known as monads. According to his view, monads are indivisible, without shape, and cannot interact with each other. Instead, they are programmed to act in a way that mirrors the rest of the universe, leading to a harmonious whole that he saw as evidence of God's wisdom.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Scientific Inventions and Discoveries</strong><br>\nBeyond philosophy and mathematics, Leibniz was an inventor and a scientist. He invented the stepped reckoner, which was an early form of a mechanical calculator. He also made discoveries in various scientific disciplines, including biology, geology, and physics. Leibniz's insatiable curiosity pushed the boundaries of what was known in his time and led to practical innovations in calculation and knowledge organization.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Work in Law and Diplomacy</strong><br>\nLeibniz was not just an academic but also an active participant in the legal and diplomatic affairs of his era. He served as a counselor in the court of Hanover and was involved in efforts to reconcile various Christian denominations. His work in law and diplomacy was an extension of his logical approach to problem-solving, as he often sought to find harmonious solutions to the conflicts of his day.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
