          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Correspondence and Collaboration</strong><br>\nLeibniz was a prolific writer and correspondent. He communicated with many of the leading intellectuals of his time, including Antoine Arnauld, Samuel Clarke, and even with Spinoza indirectly. Through these correspondences, he exchanged ideas and debated philosophical points, helping to refine his own thoughts and contribute to the intellectual growth of others.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Later Recognition</strong><br>\nLeibniz's work was not fully appreciated in his lifetime, but his reputation grew posthumously. His contributions to calculus led to prolonged disputes over priority with Newton's followers. However, today, Leibniz is recognized as one of history's greatest geniuses, with the Leibniz Association, one of Germany's largest research organizations, named after him, commemorating his broad contributions to science and philosophy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Posthumous Impact</strong><br>\nLeibniz died on November 14, 1716, in Hanover, without much public recognition. At the time of his death, Europe did not yet fully appreciate his intellectual contributions. Nevertheless, his ideas continued to influence various fields long after he passed away. Leibniz's thoughts and inventions laid the groundwork for much modern philosophy, mathematics, and computer science, and his legacy lives on in these areas.</p>\n"
              },
